    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 279,
        "text": "Hi everyone, welcome to the Science Café. I'm Maya, and tonight we're talking about whether we should trust scientific studies that get a lot of attention online. Before we start, can I ask each of you what you think is the biggest problem with science communication today?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "62e2830a385b0a023e71e24e81301fa63c24b519784259cacff0a280ced86582",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "tom",
        "speaker": "Tom",
        "sourceStart": 286,
        "sourceEnd": 643,
        "text": "Sure. I think the biggest problem is that people often don't know how to check whether a study is reliable. For example, last week I saw a headline saying that drinking coffee could help you live longer. But when I read the actual study, it only involved fifty people, and they were all over sixty. So I wasn't sure if the result really applied to everyone.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "38167b8eb3b4c67e8f5e2fe2d2217ceb7ef238b101b79b03ca9190e0ad9ee59e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 651,
        "sourceEnd": 724,
        "text": "That's a good point. So you're saying that the size of the study matters?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "6599ad61ec7d9f052b4435359492981eac891af7fbcce7f809999bf9c54921b8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "tom",
        "speaker": "Tom",
        "sourceStart": 731,
        "sourceEnd": 841,
        "text": "Exactly. And also who paid for it. If a company that sells coffee pays for the research, we should be careful.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "1cf028cc2d523c20f66b78c57e2bd220196abdbbe0604dcb6e93eee726e99b1e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 849,
        "sourceEnd": 878,
        "text": "Right. What about you, Priya?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "dd17733d4ce8db71607765cc5e3ddc41542929e8ecf2df98771e14b38b8c753e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 887,
        "sourceEnd": 1155,
        "text": "I agree with Tom, but I'd add that scientists themselves need to communicate better. Many researchers write only for other experts, so ordinary people can't understand their papers. I think universities should train scientists to explain their work in simple language.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "039738e177b5c1631f256bf95031ba48fe55c33feae0aba88991eb332e3dc4dc",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1163,
        "sourceEnd": 1210,
        "text": "So you believe that better training would help?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "b8ce97444fa676554fc7551e95de9b75b6fce78f3b9bee71ba60ccb5e164cb8a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 1219,
        "sourceEnd": 1392,
        "text": "Yes, definitely. And I also think that social media companies should do more to stop false information. But I'm not sure how they could do that without limiting free speech.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "be3d63f7bd286a54a1177b8a20e5139fc933790b0d2424b7267945fcfb09f968",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1400,
        "sourceEnd": 1475,
        "text": "That's a tricky balance. Tom, do you think social media is mainly to blame?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "16dc7069d1e727d1e5de6d9daa5e1262d720cc42051132b83514bfc3a3726848",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "tom",
        "speaker": "Tom",
        "sourceStart": 1482,
        "sourceEnd": 1719,
        "text": "Not mainly, no. I think the media in general is responsible. Newspapers often exaggerate results because they want more readers. For instance, a small study about mice might become 'New cure for cancer!' in a headline. That's misleading.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "48ee2d4ee0a9413d7e39d37678d8381f4c016294c09dfe89de2885cdf195b25f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1727,
        "sourceEnd": 1801,
        "text": "So if the media reported more accurately, would people trust science more?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "d8b842994d89f9ac737b1dcbd17cc88b8eed5c099cf49544ded327d817156874",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "tom",
        "speaker": "Tom",
        "sourceStart": 1808,
        "sourceEnd": 1981,
        "text": "Possibly, but I think people also need to learn some basic science themselves. If schools taught students how to evaluate evidence, adults would be better at judging claims.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "1ed0d75b27226c776944f0a55aab523097c14f620f3801aad20e98fb949c2aff",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 1990,
        "sourceEnd": 2185,
        "text": "I agree. And I'd say that the problem is not just about knowledge but also about emotions. People share stories that make them feel strong emotions, like fear or hope, without checking the facts.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "c132492786032e7a88f1442885aa30b80c0dec746adce967ffea772f0c76aca3",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2193,
        "sourceEnd": 2308,
        "text": "That's an interesting point. So what's one thing our listeners could do tomorrow to improve their science literacy?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "b6e0a13524d6ffa9a62251de20e0e29d90e721f1fa543cfb696bd8d06100baa1",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 2317,
        "sourceEnd": 2473,
        "text": "I'd suggest that they always ask, 'What is the original source?' before sharing a science story. And if they can't find the source, they shouldn't share it.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "ad56ed2b40737c02935bdaae5a138de856179930e2aeb944cbb710b2bf199833",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "tom",
        "speaker": "Tom",
        "sourceStart": 2480,
        "sourceEnd": 2558,
        "text": "I'd add: check the date. Sometimes old studies are shared as if they were new.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "dfe233f5a0ba0560f51013390e5d883761ad4f01b8e55ff098ef46b4ee1a3f2c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2566,
        "sourceEnd": 2632,
        "text": "Great advice. Thank you both. That's all for today's Science Café.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "03599557a1767f8feeecd1487c5e8c30ff7998eb77877b148101e455cb579b9c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      }
    ]
  },
